    [
        'slug' => 'classic-monkeys-paw',
        'gutenberg_id' => 12122,
        'title' => 'The Monkey\'s Paw',
        'author' => 'W. W. Jacobs',
        'license' => 'Public domain',
        'genres' => ['Horror'],
        'quiz_difficulty' => 'medium',
        'cover' => 'covers/classic-monkeys-paw.jpg',
        'synopsis' => 'An old charm grants three wishes to three men. The White family learns too late that fate punishes those who interfere.',
        'text' => "Outside, the night was cold and wet, but in the small parlor of Laburnum Villa the blinds were drawn and the fire burned bright. Father and son were at chess, the father holding ideas about the game that involved radical changes, putting his king into such sharp and unnecessary perils that they provoked comment from the white-haired old lady knitting placidly by the fire.\n\n\"Hark at the wind,\" said Mr. White, who had just seen a fatal mistake too late to fix it.\n\n\"I'm listening,\" said his son Herbert, grimly surveying the board as he stretched out his hand. \"Check.\"\n\nThe father wished desperately for a distraction, and it came. The gate banged and heavy footsteps approached the door. The old lady rose quickly to open it, greeting a tall, burly man with small eyes and a red face who was standing in the rain.\n\n\"Sergeant-Major Morris,\" she said, \"is it really you?\"\n\nHe shook her hand and pressed Mr. White's shoulder warmly, and after a while the three of them sat watching the fire while he told stories of wars and plagues and strange peoples. He spoke of odd scenes in India until the family's eyes grew round. Then he pushed back his chair.\n\n\"Twenty-one years of it,\" said Mr. White, nodding at his wife and son. \"When he went away he was a slip of a youth. Now look at him.\"\n\n\"He doesn't look to have taken much harm,\" said Mrs. White kindly.\n\n\"I'd like to go to India myself,\" said the old man. \"Just to look around a bit.\"\n\n\"Better where you are,\" said the sergeant-major, shaking his head. He put down his empty glass and sighed softly.\n\nAt the third glass his eyes got brighter and he began to talk again, and the little family drew close to hear about the monkey's paw that had appeared in the conversation like an unexpected guest. He had spoken loosely of charms and spells and talismans all evening, and Mr. White had teased him gently about believing in such things.\n\n\"Well, it's just a bit of what you might call magic,\" he said roughly when the question came again. He took something from his pocket and held it out. It was a dried shriveled paw with a twisted face on it. As Mr. White took it, the soldier drew back sharply.\n\n\"And what is there special about it?\" asked Mr. White as he took it.\n\n\"It had a spell put on it by an old fakir,\" said the sergeant-major. \"He was a very holy man, and he wanted to show that fate ruled people's lives, and that those who interfered with it did so to their sorrow. He put a spell on it so that three separate men could each have three wishes from it.\"\n\nMr. White looked surprised. \"Has anybody else wished?\"\n\n\"The first man had his three wishes. I don't know what the first two were, but the third was for death. That's how I got the paw.\" His voice sank low, and Mrs. White shivered.\n\n\"If you've had your three wishes, why keep it?\" asked Herbert.\n\n\"I did,\" said the soldier quietly. \"I don't know what the first two were, but I can tell you the third was for death.\"\n\nThe others laughed uncomfortably. Mr. White held the paw up and turned it around. \"It looks like the Maw and Meggins joke,\" he said. \"Suppose you wished for four pairs of hands, would anything happen?\"\n\n\"You might drop it in the fire,\" said the visitor grimly.\n\n\"How do you do it?\" asked the old man.\n\n\"Hold it up in your right hand and wish aloud,\" said the sergeant-major. \"But I warn you of the consequences.\"\n\n\"Sounds like the Arabian Nights,\" said Mrs. White, rising to lay supper things. \"Don't you think you might wish me four pairs of hands for the washing?\"\n\nHerbert took the paw and tossed it on the fire. His friend bent and threw it off with his bare hand before anyone could stop him.\n\n\"Better let it burn,\" said the soldier solemnly.\n\n\"If you don't want it, Morris,\" said the old man, \"give it to me.\"\n\n\"I won't,\" said his friend stubbornly. Then he stood to go. At the door he turned back. \"I pulled it off the fire tonight with my own hand. If you keep it, don't blame me for what happens. Be sensible. Wish for something sensible and then burn it.\"\n\nHe went out into the night, and the door shut behind him. In the parlor Mr. White sat turning the paw over in his hand.\n\n\"If you must wish,\" said Herbert, eyeing him, \"wish for two hundred pounds, father. That'll just clear the house.\"\n\nMr. White dropped the money idea sheepishly, but his fingers closed over the talisman. Herbert watched him with a broad grin, and his mother set down her work to see what would happen.\n\n\"Two hundred pounds,\" said the old man distinctly.\n\nA fine crash from the piano greeted the words, interrupted by a shuddering cry from the old man. His wife and son ran toward him.\n\n\"It moved,\" he cried, flinging it down. \"As I wished it twisted in my hand like a snake!\"\n\n\"Well, I didn't see the money,\" said Herbert, picking it up and placing it on the table, \"and I bet I never shall.\"\n\nThey sat by the dying fire and talked of strange lands, and the wind howled higher outside. The old man was shaken by his tiny disappointment. Nothing happened. The two hundred pounds did not appear, and when the family went to bed the paw sat silently under its cloth on the table.\n\nIn the brightness of the winter morning the idea seemed absurd. Herbert laughed at the whole thing as he kissed his mother and set off for work, joking that he would never see the money. His father smiled faintly and put the paw back in his pocket, shaking his head at last night's folly.\n\nMrs. White watched the road through the window all day, waiting for her son to come whistling home. Toward evening a well-dressed stranger knocked at the gate. He hesitated on the walk as if he did not know what to do, then came up to the door with quick steps.\n\n\"I come from Maw and Meggins,\" he said at last.\n\nThe old lady started. \"Is anything the matter? Has anything happened to Herbert? What is it?\"\n\nThe visitor swallowed and looked out the window. \"Your son is badly hurt,\" he said slowly, \"but he is not in any pain.\"\n\n\"Oh, thank God,\" said the old woman, clasping her hands. \"Thank God for that.\"\n\nThere was a long silence. The husband and wife hardly breathed.\n\n\"He was caught in the machinery,\" said the visitor at length, \"and they wish me to convey their sincere sympathy.\" He paused. \"The firm wishes you to understand that, without any question of liability, they make you a grant of two hundred pounds.\"\n\nOld Mr. White gave a cry and his wife fainted. The money lay on the table exactly as Herbert had joked it would be.\n\nThe burial came days later, and then nothing but gray sky and quiet rooms and the memory of a boy's laugh. The old couple crept about their home in a daze. Neighbors called and left, and the days passed one like another. Life was flat and cold. Sometimes the old man thought he heard Herbert whistling at the gate, and the silence that followed was worse than the noise.\n\nIt was more than a week afterward that the old woman woke suddenly in the night and sat upright listening. A deep sleep had fallen on her husband, and the house lay dark around her. She stretched out her hand and shook him.\n\n\"Herbert!\" she cried wildly. \"Herbert! The paw! You wished for two hundred pounds. We've only had one wish. We've still got two.\"\n\nThe old man sat up in bed and flung the bedclothes aside. \"You won't bring him back,\" he gasped. But his wife was already reaching for the candle and searching the floor below where the talisman had fallen from his pocket the night of the wish.\n\n\"Find it,\" she cried fiercely. \"Bring him back. Oh, bring him back!\"\n\nDownstairs he found the paw under a chair and carried it back trembling. Even his wife's face seemed changed to him as she bent over it.\n\n\"Wish,\" she cried.\n\nHe stood silent, pale and shaking, with the dead thing in his hand. \"He has been dead ten days,\" he muttered at last. \"And besides, I only got the idea through the wish itself. What could there be to wish now?\"\n\n\"Go and get it and wish,\" cried his wife, struggling with the terrible thought. And the old man raised his hand. \"I wish my son alive again,\" he said in a cracked voice.\n\nThe paw fell to the floor. They watched it, but it lay still. The candle flame stood straight and calm, and no sound came anywhere in the house.\n\nThen the old man sank into a chair, and a cold sweat broke out on his forehead. His wife brought him water, and they sat without speaking while the clock ticked in the corner. Somewhere below them the house creaked as old houses do, and each small sound made the old man's heart jump.\n\nSomething was moving downstairs. A slow creaking of boards reached them across the room, and then a knock sounded at the door.\n\nThe old man held his breath. The knocking came again, quiet at first. His wife rose, but he seized her arm.\n\n\"What are you going to do?\" he whispered hoarsely.\n\n\"It's my boy. It's Herbert!\" she cried, straining toward the door. \"I forgot it was two miles to the cemetery. What was it you wished?\"\n\nShe broke free and ran downstairs, and the knocking grew louder. Another knock and another, heavy and slow and patient. The lamp flickered and guttered. The bolt began to move slowly under her trembling fingers.\n\n\"Come back,\" he said tenderly. \"You will be cold.\"\n\n\"It's my boy. It's Herbert,\" she sobbed. \"I forgot it was two miles to the cemetery.\"\n\nThen the old man remembered the unpardonable thing. He fumbled on the floor for the paw and snatched it up. The knocking ceased suddenly, though he could hear his wife scraping at the bolt. He heard the chair drawn back and the door opened, and a cold wind rushed up the stairs while a long loud wail of disappointment and misery from his wife gave him courage to run down to her side.\n\n\"What's keeping you?\" she whispered breathlessly. \"Open the door, for God's sake.\"\n\nBut even as she spoke, she saw the old man's face. He stood with the paw lifted high, and his lips moved.\n\n\"What are you doing?\" she cried. \"Where is our son?\" For a moment she saw past him to the open doorway, and the street beyond was empty. The lamp flickered against the road and the quiet night. There was nothing there but the wind and the flicker of light on the empty pavement.\n\nThe old woman gave a great cry of grief and ran out into the road, calling her son's name. Her husband followed her, and together they stood in the cold air staring down the empty street. Far off a street lamp flickered and went out. There was nothing to be seen and nobody to be heard, and the night lay quiet around them.",
