        'cover' => 'covers/classic-bride-yellow-sky.jpg',
        'synopsis' => 'A prairie town marshal brings home a secret bride just as its wildest gunman turns loose looking for a fight.',
        'text' => "The great Pullman train was whirling onward with such smoothness of motion that a glance from the window seemed only to prove that the plains of Texas were pouring eastward. Vast flats of green grass, dull-hued and trailing off into the distance, swept under the sun. A long flume of wood and metal stretched beside the track, and here and there stood windmills pumping water for the thirsty land. Miles of level country unrolled without a fence or a house, and far off a line of low hills lay blue against the sky.\n\nThe newly married pair had boarded this coach in San Antonio. The man's face was reddened from many days in the wind and sun of the ranges, and he had the look of a frontier soldier. He wore new black clothes that did not fit him well, and he sat uneasy in them, for they were the garb of civilization. Every few minutes he glanced at the other passengers to see whether they noticed his stiffness, and each time he found them absorbed in their newspapers, which relieved but did not calm him.\n\nAcross from him sat his bride, who was not pretty nor was she very young. She wore a dress of blue cashmere with little braids of the same color edging its round throat, and her hat had been bought by her husband in San Antonio, where she had never before traveled. Between long silences she looked shyly out of the window and then down at the floor. Once in a while she stole a glance at her husband's stern profile and blushed at some private thought.\n\nThe man was Jack Potter, marshal of the far prairie town of Yellow Sky. He had gone east to San Antonio on business of the town, and while there he had met this woman and married her without telling anyone at home. Now he was bringing her back as if smuggling her across the border, and he felt like an outlaw returning to the scene of his crime. He had planned his entrance carefully. They would slip off the train at night, walk quietly to his house, and let the news come out later as it pleased. But the train schedule had betrayed him, and now they would arrive in broad daylight before all the town. The clear heaven overhead seemed to watch him. He wondered what the people of Yellow Sky would think when they learned their town marshal had taken a wife without asking anyone.\n\nThe porter, a black man in a white jacket, came through the car and announced dinner. The couple rose stiffly. They had eaten nothing since the wedding morning except some fruit and cake.\n\nIn the dining car the tables shone with linen and glass, and waiters moved quietly between them. The pair took seats near a window, feeling the gaze of other diners upon their rough clothes. The bride unfolded her napkin and studied the array of knives and forks before her with anxious eyes, arranging them as best she could guess. The waiter brought soup, and she tasted it carefully. Her husband watched her with tender concern and spoke in low tones meant to comfort her.\n\nThe silver dishes, the little salt spoons, the way the waiter poured water without being asked, all these things were marvels and mysteries to her. She had grown up where meals were eaten from tin plates, and this glittering room made her feel like a stranger at a king's table. Yet slowly the warmth of the meal reached them. The husband began to talk of Yellow Sky, of the wide dusty street, of the wooden stores with false fronts, of the men who would tip their hats to her. He told her how fine a thing it was that he, the marshal, should come home a married man. But even as he spoke, doubt returned to his face, for he knew that in Yellow Sky a marriage was a public event, and he had made it private.\n\nWhile the train fled onward through the afternoon, another scene waited at the end of the line.\n\nIn Yellow Sky the sun blazed over a street of hard sand. Before one of the saloons stood a group of men talking idly, and a dog dozed in the dust between the rails of the wagon road. Suddenly the door of the saloon burst open and a young man leaned out and shouted up the street in a voice of alarm.\n\n\"Scratchy Wilson is drunk,\" he cried. \"He has turned loose in the whole town.\"\n\nNo one moved quickly, for there was nothing unusual in this news. Men looked at each other with mild interest, the way townsfolk look when a familiar storm is reported coming. Inside the saloon the barkeeper polished glasses and said nothing. The young messenger ran up the street shouting his warning, and shutters began to close along the wooden buildings. Women drew back behind doors. The dog slunk away.\n\nA drummer, a traveling salesman with sample cases, stood puzzled before the darkened windows. He caught the arm of a man hurrying past. \"What is it? What's the matter?\"\n\n\"Scratchy Wilson,\" said the man shortly, \"and he's drunk.\"\n\n\"Well, what of it?\"\n\n\"What of it?\" The citizen stared at him. Then he explained that Scratchy Wilson was looking for a fight, that he shot at the doorways of his enemies, and that when he was in this condition no sensible man went out to meet him. Twice before, men had faced him, and one of those affairs had ended badly for everyone.\n\n\"But does nobody fight him?\" asked the drummer.\n\n\"Not today,\" said the other grimly. \"There's only one man in town who ever faces him regular, and that's Jack Potter, our marshal. And Potter's away in San Antonio on business.\"\n\n\"Is he dangerous?\" asked the salesman, peering nervously up the empty street.\n\n\"He's a terror when he's drunk,\" was the answer. \"Last time he turned loose he shot up every window on this street and put a hole through the hat of a man who laughed at him.\"\n\nThe drummer stood alone in the empty street after his informant had gone. From somewhere beyond the stores came the sound of shots, quick and irregular, and then the strange voice of Scratchy Wilson singing a song of the war, sung with wild yells between the verses. The salesman pressed himself against a wall and wished heartily that he had chosen another route for his journey.\n\nPresently the shooting stopped. Scratchy Wilson, having exhausted the town's doorways, remembered the house of the absent marshal. He staggered down a side lane, carrying two revolvers which he handled with the tenderness of a mother lifting her babies. At Potter's gate he stopped and called out his challenge in a voice that rolled over the quiet houses.\n\nHe waited. No answer came. The house sat dumb in the sunlight, its shutters closed against him. He called again, cursing the silence, and prowled around the little fence like a hungry animal. He shouted insults at the empty windows and dared the absent owner to come out and die like a man. Finding no enemy, he turned back toward the center of town, still seeking somebody to fight, and his shadow staggered along beside him over the hot sand.\n\nAt this moment Jack Potter and his bride were walking up the main street of Yellow Sky. They had left the station together, the bride carrying her small trunk of belongings, and the marshal pointed out the landmarks as they passed. There was the church where he hoped they would be welcomed. Beyond it stood his own house, plain and weather-beaten, waiting now for its mistress.\n\nAs they walked they heard a sound behind them, a curious sliding step in the sand. The marshal turned, and his face went white under its bronze.\n\nBefore them stood Scratchy Wilson, swaying gently, his boots planted wide apart in the dust. His red hair stood out fiercely above his staring eyes, and his jaw hung like that of a wild beast about to spring. In his right hand gleamed one of the revolvers. He was clothed in the costume of an old-time gunman, a flannel shirt and heavy boots, and he looked like a creature let loose from an earlier and wilder age, some huge cave bear roused out of sleep to prowl the town.\n\n\"Here we go,\" he said softly, with a smile of terrible pleasure. \"Here we go again, Jack Potter. I know you're game. Put up your hands.\"\n\nThe bride gave a little cry and clutched her husband's arm.\n\nPotter stepped slightly in front of her. His mind raced through all the codes of the border country. He knew the etiquette of such meetings as well as his enemy did. A marshal who faced a gunman without a weapon was a fool, yet one who ran was finished forever in Yellow Sky. He saw the pistol rising level toward him and thought with strange clearness of the dining car he had left an hour ago, and how far away it now seemed.\n\n\"I ain't got a gun on me, Scratchy,\" he said hoarsely. \"Honest I haven't.\"\n\n\"You know I'll fight you, Scratchy,\" he went on quickly. \"You know I will. But not now. Not like this.\"\n\n\"Not now?\" sneered the gunman. \"Not like this?\" He laughed wildly. \"Well, fix yourself up then. Go get your gun and come out. I'll wait.\"\n\n\"It's different this time, Scratchy,\" said the marshal. He swallowed hard. \"I'm married. This here is my wife. We were just coming from San Antonio, and I haven't got a gun because I didn't need one traveling.\"\n\nThe gunman stared at the woman as if seeing her for the first time. His mouth hung open and his fierce eyes slowly lost their fire.\n\n\"Married,\" he repeated dully.\n\n\"Yes, married,\" said Potter firmly. \"This is my wife, Scratchy.\"\n\nThe point struck deep. It broke through all the machinery of his rage. Scratchy Wilson stood in the sand with his revolver drooping, blinking at the little blue-dressed woman holding her husband's arm. All his life the code of the fight had ruled him, and here was something the code had no answer for. A man could not shoot at a married couple walking home from the train. Somewhere far off in his confused mind he understood that the world had changed while he was not watching.\n\nHe lowered the revolver until it pointed at the ground. His shoulders sagged.\n\n\"Well,\" he said at last, and his voice was almost gentle, \"I s'pose it's all off if there ain't a gun on the street.\"\n\nAnd he turned and walked slowly away down the road, leaving the marshal and his wife standing in the dust of Yellow Sky.",
