    [
        'slug' => 'classic-build-a-fire',
        'gutenberg_id' => 2429,
        'title' => 'To Build a Fire',
        'author' => 'Jack London',
        'license' => 'Public domain',
        'genres' => ['Adventure'],
        'quiz_difficulty' => 'hard',
        'cover' => 'covers/classic-build-a-fire.jpg',
        'synopsis' => 'A newcomer crosses the frozen Yukon with his dog. One wet foot and one failed match teach him what fifty below really means.',
        'text' => "Day had broken cold and gray, exceedingly cold and gray, when the man turned aside from the main Yukon trail and climbed a high earth bank where a dim little path led east through the tall spruce timber. The bank was steep, and he paused at the top to catch his breath. It was nine o'clock. There was no sun or promise of sun, though the sky was clear and bright and there was no wind. He did not mind. He was used to having no sun in the sky during these long winter months far north of the Arctic Circle.\n\nIt was fifty degrees below zero. The man knew this only because he had measured it the night before at the fork of the creek, and because the thermometer was fixed in his mind as the true cold of the season. He was a newcomer to the land, a chechaquo, and this was his first winter. The trouble with him was that he was without imagination. He was quick and alert in the things of life, but only in those things, and not in their meanings. Fifty degrees below zero meant exactly that to him, a number, nothing more. It did not lead him to think of his own weakness as a creature of warmth, nor of man's small place against the great cold of the universe. He was just a man traveling light and fast toward camp, and the cold was simply cold.\n\nHe was bound for an old claim on the left fork of Henderson Creek, where the boys were already. They had come over across the divide from the Indian Creek country, while he had taken the roundabout way to look at the possibility of floating logs from the islands in spring. He would arrive at camp at six o'clock in the evening, a bit late perhaps, but the boys would be there and a fire would be burning and supper would be hot.\n\nThe trail was faint. Not once had he seen a sled track all day. A foot of snow had fallen since the last sled went over, and he was glad he traveled without a sled now. In truth he carried little, lunch wrapped in a handkerchief under one arm, and matches and some birch bark in a pocket. On top of that he wore moccasins too thin for such walking but good for quick movement. He had expected a trail into Henderson Creek where the trees stood thick, but everything was covered in white, and each mile he walked seemed to him exactly like every other mile.\n\nAhead of him trotted a large gray native wolf dog, its coat thick with hidden dampness that froze and gathered in crystals on the hair. The fine frost crystals hung like jewels above its muzzle and around its jaw while it breathed, and they sparkled in the strange light whenever it lifted its head. The dog was depressed by the tremendous cold. It knew that this was no time for traveling. Its instinct told it a truer tale than any number the man could read off a glass tube. The animal was frightened by the cold, so deep a fright that it refused to go forward into the open spaces between the trees until the man insisted. But the man pressed on, whistling to it, and it followed behind at his heels.\n\nOnce the dog broke through one of these hidden springs itself and wet its paws. It licked the ice from between its toes quickly, for it knew better than the man what such wetness meant at this temperature. The man paid no attention. He pushed straight ahead across another of these places himself, and the crackling gave way beneath him. He sank down ankle deep in icy water, swore softly, and climbed out onto firmer ground. That would delay him an hour, for he would have to build a fire and dry out his footgear.\n\nAt such temperatures life can exist only directly against fire. To stand still was to invite death. So he ran on, feeling the sting of frost begin in his toes. Then came noon. The man sat by a fine spruce tree and took out his lunch. He smiled pleasantly as he thought of eating biscuits with bacon slices. The dog nestled close, begging, and he shared a bite or two with it. Afterward he pulled off his mittens and built a small fire, and the heat was pleasant. While it burned he munched and thought of nothing much. Then he put on his mittens again, tucked the handkerchief away, and stood up. The dog swung around at his heels, ready for the trail.\n\nThe man continued to follow the faint path. For some time the ghostly silence of the land held him. It was very still, and the world seemed made of white and gray. He walked briskly, and the blood pulsed warm under his clothing. Then suddenly he broke through another hidden spring, this one deeper than the first. Before he could catch himself, the water was up to his knees. He cursed his luck aloud and turned instantly toward the trees, for he knew that here he must stop and build a fire.\n\nHe was angry at himself for being so foolish, but he worked quickly and calmly. First he gathered a supply of twigs and branches, then larger sticks, and laid them near at hand. He stripped the bark from a dead birch and made a pile of dry shavings. Into these he crumbled some flakes of the bark itself, and struck a match. The flame leaped up eagerly, and he fed it small twigs until a roaring fire stood up between the trees. He was safe now. Feet and hands would soon be warm and dry.\n\nThe fire was a success. He was comfortable now, sitting on a flat rock, drying his socks and moccasins by turns. He even laughed at the old-timer on Sulphur Creek who had given him advice the fall before. The old man had said that no man must travel alone in the Klondike after fifty below. Well, here he was, he had broken through twice and gotten his feet wet, and here he was now, alive and warm. Any man who was a man could travel alone.\n\nThen it happened. High up in the tree the fire had come from, the wind moved a bough. Down came the load of snow that had gathered there all week. It fell without warning upon the man and the fire, and the blazing grass and twigs were smothered under the white weight. Where the fire had been was a heap of fresh powder, and the warmth died out of the air.\n\nThe man stared at the place where the fire had been. He was shocked, but not yet afraid. If he built another fire quickly, all would be well. His feet were wet and numb already, but he could still feel them, and the thought of losing them did not yet seem real. He remembered the old-timer's warning now, and for the first time it carried weight.\n\nHe turned to gather wood, and here his trouble began. His fingers were so stiff that they could hardly bend, and every twig he touched broke off with a snap. The cold had crept in while the first fire burned, and it had taken a firm hold of him. He forced himself to work slowly, picking up twigs one at a time with both hands, laying them down where he could reach them again. Slowly he assembled a new foundation of wood, and reached into his pocket for the matches.\n\nThe matches were in a small paper packet, and his fingers would not close on it. He tried again and again, and then bent his head down and managed to grip the packet between his forearm and the crook of two stiffened fingers. He tore it open with a jerk. There were seventy sulphur matches in it, and he knew he might never get another chance like this. He tried to pick out one match, but the whole bunch came loose together and fell into the snow. He caught at them wildly and brought them up in a frozen wad.\n\nHe placed the bundle of matches against his knee and drew his knife, meaning to strike them on the blade. But the flesh of his hands refused to obey him, and the knife slipped away into the snow. Then, in a burst of desperation, he struck the whole bunch of matches at once against his leg. Seventy matches flared together in a single blaze, hotter than one alone, and the smoke rolled up in a thick cloud. He held the burning mass against the birch bark and fed it with tiny twigs from his wood pile, but the pain of his scorching flesh broke his grip. The matches fell into the snow, and a gust of wind fanned the little flame he had made. It flickered, sank, and died.\n\nThe man sat staring before him. This was terrible. His feet and hands were freezing, and he had no way to make a fire. He rose to his feet in a panic. All his knowledge of the north, all the warnings he had ignored, came crowding back. The old-timer on Sulphur Creek was right, and he had laughed at the advice. There was no arguing with the cold. It did not care whether a man was strong or brave or quick. It simply killed whatever stood too long against it.\n\nA wild thought seized him. He would kill the dog and bury his hands in the warm body until feeling came back, and then build another fire. He called the dog to him, but his voice had a strange terror in it, and the animal sensed that something was wrong. It backed away. At last he got close enough to seize it, but his hands hung useless at his sides. He could not take out his knife. He fought with his own frozen body, twisting and straining, and lost. The dog sprang away and stood watching him from a distance, uncertain what this strange madness might be.\n\nThen the man ran. He dashed blindly down the creek, falling and rising, hoping against hope to reach the camp and the boys. His legs were heavy logs, and his lungs breathed sharp needles of air. The wild flight did him good, for it drove the blood warmer through his body for a time. He ran on and on, but the trail seemed endless, and the trees stood just as thick as ever. Then a new fear came upon him, the fear of falling down in the snow and never rising again.\n\nHalfway there he fell. The snow was soft and he lay where he fell, feeling strangely comfortable. A great tiredness crept over him. To run on meant certain death from freezing, but to lie still meant sleep, and his whole being cried out for sleep. The warmth of rest pulled at him gently, promising peace if only he would lie still. It was very sweet to lie there.\n\nHe struggled up once more and staggered on. His mind had begun to work strangely now, and the thought of death no longer frightened him. Then he fell again, and this time he stayed down. Somewhere in his fading mind he pictured the old men of the camp finding his body on the second day, frozen stiff beside the trail, and the old-timer on Sulphur Creek saying that he had warned him. The man smiled a little at that. Yes, the old man had been right. He had been foolish, and he paid the price. He drifted into a warm and friendly sleep, and the cold no longer mattered.\n\nThe dog sat by him a long time, waiting. As the evening darkened it came closer and smelled the strange stillness of the man. Something warned it, and its hair bristled. It lifted its head and howled. Then it turned and trotted up the trail in the direction of the camp it knew, where were the other food providers and fire providers.",
