        'synopsis' => 'A lazy farmer escapes his scolding wife for a day of hunting and wakes twenty years later in a world turned upside down.',
        'text' => "Whoever has made a voyage up the Hudson River must remember the Kaatskill mountains. They are a broken branch of the great Appalachian family, seen away to the west of the river, swelling up to a noble height and looking down over the surrounding country. Every change of season, every change of weather, indeed every hour of the day, makes some change in their magical colors and shapes. All the good wives far and near regard them as perfect weather signs, and the old Dutch inhabitants almost believe them to be the home of strange spirits.\n\nAt the foot of these fairy mountains lies a little village of old Dutch houses with yellow gables, built by the first settlers of the province. In that same village, and in one of its very oldest houses, lived a good-natured fellow named Rip Van Winkle. He was a descendant of a famous line of brave Dutchmen, but he had little of his ancestors' fighting spirit left in him.\n\nRip was a kind neighbor and an obedient husband at home. The children of the village shouted with joy whenever he approached. He helped everyone with every job except his own farm. He built fences for his neighbors, he shucked their corn, he ran errands to the mill for the whole village. Nobody's business went wrong because of Rip, except his own. His fences were falling down, his cow wandered off to eat his neighbor's cabbages, his fields grew weeds instead of wheat, and his children ran wild in ragged clothes. His son, little Rip, dressed just like his father and was ready to follow in his footsteps as faithfully as a young tree follows the shape of the fence it leans on.\n\nThe truth of the matter was that Dame Van Winkle kept a continual watch upon everything her husband did. Morning, noon, and night her tongue was going, and everything he said or did was sure to produce a torrent of household speech. Rip had but one way of replying to all lectures on the subject, which was by shrugging his shoulders and shaking his head and looking wise, saying nothing. This, however, only made matters worse.\n\nRip had one great comfort. Whenever things went badly at home, he would take his gun and whistle for his dog Wolf, and stroll away into the woods. Wolf was as much henpecked as his master, for Dame Van Winkle looked upon the dog as an accomplice in her husband's idleness, and often threw things at him. In the forest they were both welcome. Rip declared that even Wolf's tail drooped whenever he turned toward home.\n\nOne fine autumn day Rip had climbed to one of the highest points of the Kaatskills with his gun and his dog. He hunted squirrel and wild pigeon through the afternoon until the sun began to sink behind the blue ridges. Panting and breathless, he threw himself on a green knoll crowned with mountain ash. From this height he could see the whole lower Hudson country spread out below, and the deep quiet of the wilderness came over him. He sighed. It was a fine place for peace, thought he, far from the noise of a scolding tongue.\n\nHe was about to descend when he heard a voice from the hollow calling his name. \"Rip Van Winkle! Rip Van Winkle!\" He looked around but could see nothing but a crow winging its flight across the mountain. He turned again to descend, and heard the same cry ring through the still evening air. \"Rip Van Winkle! Rip Van Winkle!\" At the same moment Wolf bristled up his back and, giving a low growl, skulked to his master's side in fear.\n\nRip now felt a vague dread stealing over him. He looked anxiously in the same direction and perceived a strange figure slowly toiling up the rocks. It was a short old man with thick bushy hair and a grizzled beard, dressed in antique Dutch clothing. He carried on his shoulder a stout keg that seemed full of liquor, and he made signs for Rip to come and help him with the load.\n\nRip, being naturally a helpful soul, though somewhat afraid of this odd companion, took the keg upon his own shoulder without a word, and together they began climbing a narrow ravine. From time to time the stranger rolled thunderous sounds out of his throat that echoed along the mountains, and Rip could make nothing of them. At last they reached a small hollow like an amphitheater in the mountainside.\n\nThere, to his wonder and fear, he saw a company of odd-looking personages playing at ninepins among the rocks. They were dressed in quaint outlandish fashion, some with doublets and jerkins of ancient cut, and all wore beards of various shapes and colors. The whole group reminded him of the figures in an old Flemish painting. What seemed particularly odd was that these men kept a most grave and silent air, and the only voice heard was the rolling thunder of the balls as they crashed among the rocks like peals of lightning.\n\nThe stranger led the way to a large grass-grown space where these personages were bowling. He made signs for Rip to serve out the liquor from the keg. The company now began their refreshment, and Rip trembled with fear as they passed the flagon around, but none of them spoke a single word. His fears were soon quieted. He took a hearty draught himself, and then another, and another. At last his eyes swam, his head nodded, and he fell into a deep sleep.\n\nOn waking, he found himself lying on the same green knoll where he had first seen the old man with the keg. He rubbed his eyes. It was a fine sunny morning. \"Surely,\" thought Rip, \"I have not slept here all night.\" He remembered the strange men and the keg, but where were they? Where was Wolf? He whistled for his dog, whistled until his mouth rang with the sound, but no dog came.\n\nHe rose to his feet and found his joints stiff and his body strangely aching. Then a great surprise met him. Instead of the clean well-oiled fowling piece he had carried up the mountain, he found an old firelock lying by him, its barrel encrusted with rust, its lock falling off, and the stock worm-eaten. His heart grew heavy. Had the mountain spirits stolen his gun and left this useless old barrel in exchange?\n\nHe stroked his beard, hoping to wake himself from the dream, and started back with alarm. His beard had grown a foot long, gray and shaggy. The whole mountainside was changed. Strange names were over the wells, strange faces at every window. He hurried down to the village, where he stopped short with terror. Men were standing before a building he did not know, arguing about an election, shouting words he had never heard before, such as rights of citizens and members of Congress.\n\nA brisk-looking gentleman came up and seized him by the arm. \"Which party do you belong to,\" asked this worthy man, \"the federal or the democrat?\" Rip was equally at a loss before either question. Another short busy fellow broke in, dragging him along. \"Where is your gun?\" he cried, \"and where did you get it?\" Poor Rip had no answer but to say that it was his own gun, left on the mountain that morning. His tale of the mountain spirits drew nothing but suspicion from the crowd.\n\nThe village itself was larger, with more houses and people whom he knew nothing of. Faces he remembered were gone. The very dogs seemed strangers, and they barked at his heels as he passed. His own home had gone to decay, the roof fallen in, the windows shattered, and the door off its hinges. An old yellow dog hid among the ruins of what had been his fine kennel, and growled at him as he entered the yard. He called for his wife and children, and the lonely chambers rang for a moment with his voice, and then all was silence again. He hurried out into the street in despair, feeling sure that even himself had been changed by the mountain magic.\n\nThe inn he used to visit was gone too. In its place stood a tall wooden hotel with a swinging sign painted in new letters. Where the head of King George used to hang, General Washington now looked down in blue and buff. A crowd of folk thronged the porch, and when Rip asked them where Nicholas Vedder the landlord had gone, they stared at him in wonder. An orator among them demanded what brought him to the election with a gun on his shoulder and a mob at his heels, and whether he meant to breed a riot in the village.\n\n\"Alas, gentlemen,\" cried Rip, somewhat dismayed, \"I am a poor quiet man, a native of the place, and a loyal subject of the king, God bless him.\"\n\nHere a general shout burst from the bystanders. \"A tory! A spy! Away with him!\" It was with great difficulty that the self-important man in the crowd restored order. Rip's heart died away at hearing these strange words, which meant nothing to him. He begged them earnestly to tell him whether anyone knew Nicholas Vedder.\n\nAn old woman cried out from the crowd that she knew him now. It was Rip Van Winkle, returned after twenty years of absence, in which everyone had given him up for lost. She pointed to a good-looking young woman with a chubby child in her arms who was staring up at the ragged bearded man as if he had dropped from the clouds.\n\nThe young woman came near and looked at him through her tears. Her father, she said softly, had gone up the mountain with his gun one day long ago and never come back. A dog had returned alone, but the man was never heard of again. She still kept a tender memory of him, and her heart grew soft whenever she thought of the day he taught her to fly a kite on the village green.\n\n\"What is your name, my good woman?\" asked he.\n\n\"Judith Gardenier,\" she answered.\n\n\"And your father's name?\"\n\n\"Ah, poor man, Rip Van Winkle was his name, but twenty years ago he went away with his gun, and never has been heard of since. His dog came home without him, and whether he shot himself or was carried away by the Indians nobody can tell.\"\n\nRip caught his daughter and her child in his arms. \"I am your father,\" cried he. \"Young Rip Van Winkle once, old Rip Van Winkle now. Does nobody know poor Rip Van Winkle?\"\n\nAll stood amazed until an old man, Peter Vanderdonk, the most ancient inhabitant of the village, declared that it was Rip himself, and related how Hendrick Hudson, the first discoverer of the river and country, kept a kind of vigil there every twenty years with his crew of the Half Moon, keeping guardian watch over the mountains. Rip's tale, told plainly and simply, was received with ready belief, for it matched the oldest traditions of the valley.\n\nAs for Dame Van Winkle, she had lately broken a blood vessel in a fit of temper at a New England peddler, so there was peace at last. Rip's daughter took him home to live with her. His son, true image of his father, was seen leaning against trees by the roadside instead of working. Rip resumed his old walks and habits, took his place again on the bench at the hotel entrance, and was reverenced as one of the patriarchs of the village. He told his story to every stranger who arrived, and not a man, woman, or child in the neighborhood but knew it by heart. Some always pretended to doubt the reality of it, but the old Dutch inhabitants regarded it as certain truth, and even today, whenever they hear a summer storm rolling about the Kaatskills, they say that Hudson and his crew are out at their ninepins again.",
        'questions' => [
