        'synopsis' => 'A wild mother fox outwits every trap on the Seton farm, until her last cub is chained in the barnyard as bait.',
        'text' => "When I was a boy on the old Seton farm, the woods behind our orchard held a mystery that ruled our whole summer. It began with hens. Every few nights another hen vanished from our yard, and no lock or latch seemed able to stop it. The thief left tracks like little crosses in the dust, and my father said at once what we already knew. A fox was living in the wooded ravine beyond the hill.\n\nWe saw her at last one evening from the barn window. She was a red fox, slim and quick as a shadow, and she moved across the moonlit snow of the yard without a sound. Our dog was after her in a moment, baying wildly, but she slipped away through the fence gaps and led him such a chase over the hills that he came home at midnight with his pads worn sore and his pride badly hurt.\n\nHer cleverness at our expense became almost a joke in the neighborhood. She learned to open the henhouse door by working the latch with her nose, and when my father nailed it shut she dug under the corner instead. She always carried her prize off alive, holding the hen gently by the neck so that it never cried out and woke the dog. Once she took a dozen birds in a single night, hiding most of them in the brush along the creek, for foxes lay up stores like careful housekeepers.\n\nThat was how we came to know the Springfield fox, though for a long time we knew only her work and not her life. She was old and wise and had a history written all over her. One ear was torn from some ancient battle with a dog, and there was a scar on her flank where buckshot had bitten years before. The farmers round about spoke of her with anger, but I noticed something in their voices that was close to respect. She robbed them every month, yet none of them ever caught her.\n\nMy father set his traps along her trails, and she stepped around them neatly. He waited up nights with his gun, and she came by other ways. Once she passed within twenty feet of the window where he sat in the dark, and carried off a fat hen under his very eyes. She could tell a man's shape from any stump, and she never crossed an open field when she could travel a fence line. If dogs pressed her hard, she ran among the sheep in the pasture until their smell confused the trail, and sometimes she waded up a creek for a quarter mile and left her pursuers casting about in circles. Twice she led them straight through the middle of a grazing herd of cattle and doubled back while they blundered among the horns and heels.\n\nIn early spring her raids stopped almost entirely, and we learned why before long. On the warm slope above the ravine, hidden under a tangle of roots and brush, she was raising a family. My brother found the den by watching her flight path one gray morning, for a fox returning from a raid travels light and fast, while a mother returning to her young travels heavy with milk and cannot hide her road home.\n\nWe used to climb the hill quietly and lie flat in the grass a long stone's throw away, and watch the whole wild family through the long bright evenings. There were four cubs, brownish yellow balls of fur with black noses and small sharp faces. They played like kittens, tumbling over one another and biting their own tails, and whenever their mother appeared they rushed upon her with little cries of joy. The old fox would lie down in the sunshine near them, and the cubs climbed all over her, tugging her ears and tail, and she bore it all with the tired patience of every mother since the world began.\n\nShe was a different creature up here than the bold thief of our henhouse. With her children she was gentle and patient and endlessly careful. She brought them mice first, then young gophers and ground squirrels, alive and unhurt, and turned them loose near the den for the cubs to practice upon. When the prey escaped into the grass, as it often did, the old fox sat down and watched her blundering children with an air of calm amusement. Then she would show them how it was done herself, catching the runaway in two leaps and laying it down again before them.\n\nDay by day the lessons grew harder. She taught them to listen with the whole body, to freeze at the snap of a twig, to follow scent against wind and with it. She taught them the farmyard was rich and dangerous, and I believe she taught each cub its own nature too, for one was bold and one was timid and one was sly, and these characters showed plainly even in their play.\n\nThe boldest cub was my favorite. He would march straight at the biggest grasshopper and the strangest danger alike, while his timid brother ran for the den mouth. The sly one watched everything from behind a tussock before he joined in anything. Their mother never punished these differences but worked with them, sending each cub on the errand that suited its nature, and I have wondered since how much of what men call cleverness in grown foxes is only this early schooling carried on.\n\nWe kept our watching place secret from my father, partly because he would have ended it with a shovel and partly because we half feared what he might do if he learned exactly where the family lived. But boys cannot hide a thing like a fox den from a farmer forever, especially when hens keep disappearing all summer long.\n\nBut the summer wore on, and the fox grew bolder as her cubs grew hungrier. The henhouse losses began again, and my father's anger rose. He made a study of her visits and found that she always came at the same hour of the night and always left by the same gap in the fence. One evening he was waiting there with a loaded gun instead of his usual place by the window.\n\nWe heard the single shot from the house. In the morning we found blood on the fence boards and a tuft of red fur, but no fox and no hen. She had dropped her prize at the crack of the gun and gone off unhurt, and for weeks afterward she kept clear of our yard altogether. My father cleaned his gun with satisfaction, but I noticed that even he spoke more softly of her after that night. It is one thing to shoot at a thief and another to miss a creature that has outwitted you a hundred times.\n\nIt was not long, though, before hunger overcame caution. Her four cubs were nearly grown now, and they needed more meat than the ravine could give. My father took to watching the den hill instead of the henhouse, and one August morning he found their playground empty of watchers and dug the den open with his spade.\n\nI did not go with him that day, and I have been glad ever since that I did not. Three of the cubs were killed there on the hillside, quickly enough, and the fourth, the boldest one, was brought home alive and chained by the neck in the barnyard. My father's plan was simple and cruel in the way of practical men. The old fox would not leave her young one, and where she came for it, sooner or later a gun would be waiting.\n\nShe came that very night. We heard the chain rattle in the dark, and in the morning we found two mice lying beside the cub, dead fresh, such food as its mother had carried all summer. Every night after that she came, sometimes with mice, sometimes with young birds, and she laid the food down close to the little prisoner and worked at the chain until dawn. She gnawed at the iron links and scratched the earth away around the stake and pulled till her gums bled, but chain and stake were too strong for any teeth.\n\nOne night she tried a different plan altogether. She caught up the cub by the scruff of its neck and dragged it bodily toward the fence, chain and stake and all, plowing a deep furrow in the yard through half the night. By morning she had moved it six feet, and there the stake had jammed against a stone and held. She sat beside her work panting when the east grew light, and then slipped away as the men's voices rose in the house.\n\nMy father never got his shot. She was too wise to cross the open yard while any shape waited in the shadows, and after a few failures he gave up trying and let the strange visits go on. I used to lie awake listening for the soft rattle of the chain, and it made me feel strange and sad without knowing why. The cub itself seemed to understand that the visits were its whole world now, and it watched for the dark the way it once had watched for its mother's step on the den hill.\n\nThe nights turned cold in September. The cubs that had escaped my father's spade were scattered by then, gone off to hunt alone as young foxes must, and only the chained prisoner waited in our yard. One evening frost came down hard from the north country, the first true cold of the fall, and the cub lay out on the bare ground with nothing between it and the sky. In the morning my brother found it stiff and still beside its supper, which its mother had brought too late.\n\nShe came again that night, as she had come every night. She found the little body and smelled it all over, and understood what had happened. She licked the cold fur once or twice and arranged the food she had brought neatly under its nose, as if hoping against hope that the smell of supper might bring it back to life. Then she sat still in the moonlight a long time, quite silent, looking at the little dead thing she could no longer warm.\n\nAnd then she went away across the fields toward the north woods, quietly as she had come, and she never troubled our farmyard again. We saw her tracks once more that winter, far up the creek beyond the hill, traveling alone, and after that the snows held no trace of her anywhere. But I have often thought since that of all the wild creatures I ever watched, none bore itself better in trouble than the Springfield fox.",
